[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[<->] (-0.6,0)--(4.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-0.6)--(0,4.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick] (0,0)--(3.5,4.6);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Does the graph shown represent direct variation, inverse variation, or neither?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[<->] (-0.6,0)--(4.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-0.6)--(0,4.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,domain=0.28:4.3,smooth,samples=60,variable=\t] plot ({\t},{1.2/\t});
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Does the graph shown represent direct variation, inverse variation, or neither?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[<->] (-0.6,0)--(4.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-0.6)--(0,4.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick] (0,3.6)--(3.0,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Does the graph shown represent direct variation, inverse variation, or neither?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[<->] (-0.6,0)--(4.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-0.6)--(0,4.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,red] (0,0)--(4,3.6);
\node[red] at (3.0,2.9) {$A$};
\draw[thick,blue,domain=0.32:4.4,smooth,samples=60,variable=\t] plot ({\t},{1.4/\t});
\node[blue] at (0.9,2.1) {$B$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Graphs $A$ and $B$ are shown on the same axes.
Which graph could represent inverse variation?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis x line=bottom, axis y line=left, xlabel={Time $t$ (hours)}, ylabel={Speed $s$ (km/h)}, xmin=0, xmax=11, ymin=0, ymax=105, xtick={2,4,6,8,10}, ytick={20,40,60,80,100}, grid=both, samples=100, domain=1.5:10, restrict y to domain=0:105, unbounded coords=jump]
\addplot[thick,blue] {150/x};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The speed $s$ (km/h) needed to complete a $150$ km trip in time $t$ (hours) is related by the graph shown.
Use the graph to find the speed needed to complete the trip in $3$ hours.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis x line=bottom, axis y line=left, xlabel={Volume $V$ (m$^3$)}, ylabel={Pressure $P$ (kPa)}, xmin=0, xmax=19, ymin=0, ymax=19, xtick={3,6,9,12,15,18}, ytick={3,6,9,12,15,18}, grid=both, samples=100, domain=3:18, restrict y to domain=0:19, unbounded coords=jump]
\addplot[thick,blue] {54/x};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The pressure $P$ (kPa) of a fixed amount of gas and its volume $V$ (m$^3$) are related by the graph shown.
Use the graph to find the volume when the pressure is $9$ kPa.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[<->] (-0.6,0)--(4.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-0.6)--(0,4.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick] (0,3.6) arc (90:0:3.6);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
A student says this graph could show inverse variation because it is a decreasing curve.
Is the student correct?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis x line=bottom, axis y line=left, xlabel={Resistance $R$ (ohms)}, ylabel={Current $I$ (A)}, xmin=0, xmax=21, ymin=0, ymax=16, xtick={4,8,12,16,20}, ytick={2,4,6,8,10,12,14}, grid=both, samples=100, domain=2:20, restrict y to domain=0:16, unbounded coords=jump]
\addplot[thick,blue] {30/x};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Use the graph to estimate the current when the resistance is $7$ ohms. Give your estimate to one decimal place.
